var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Reading","page":"API","text":"","title":"Reading"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Accessing-and-traversing","page":"API","text":"","title":"Accessing and traversing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Topology","page":"API","text":"","title":"Topology"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Semantics","page":"API","text":"","title":"Semantics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Transformations","page":"API","text":"","title":"Transformations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Comparing","page":"API","text":"","title":"Comparing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Inspection","page":"API","text":"","title":"Inspection"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#CGMESParser.CGMESParser","page":"API","text":"CGMESParser\n\nReader and inspection tools for CGMES (Common Grid Model Exchange Standard) datasets.\n\nA CIMDataset is a faithful in-memory image of a directory of CGMES profile files: objects keep the class names and property names from the XML, and the references between them are resolved in both directions so the model can be walked as a graph. On top of that sit three optional layers — cleanup transformations, the topological split into buses and branches, and a semantic layer that reads per-unit voltages and injections.\n\nPlotting lives in a package extension; load GraphMakie together with a Makie backend to get inspect_collection and friends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.CGMESParser"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.CIMDataset","page":"API","text":"CIMDataset(directory::String)\n\nRead a whole CGMES dataset: every XML file in directory becomes a CIMFile, and the references between all of them are resolved.\n\nThis is a faithful image of what the files say. Nothing is cleaned up and nothing is interpreted — see the transformations and split_topologically for that.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.CIMDataset"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.CIMFile","page":"API","text":"CIMFile(filepath::String)\n\nRead a single CGMES profile file. References to objects in other profiles stay unresolved until the file is part of a CIMDataset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.CIMFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.CIMCollection","page":"API","text":"CIMCollection\n\nA bag of objects and extensions with no file structure attached. Subgraphs produced by split_topologically are collections, as is the result of flattening a CIMDataset with CIMCollection(dataset).\n\nmetadata is a free-form dict that the topology functions use to record things like which buses a branch connects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.CIMCollection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.CIMObject","page":"API","text":"CIMObject\n\nA single object out of a CGMES file, such as a Terminal or an ACLineSegment. It keeps the class name and the properties verbatim as they appear in the XML; nothing is renamed or converted.\n\nProperty values are either plain values or CIMRefs pointing at other objects. obj[\"SomeProperty\"] follows a reference and gives you the object on the other end, and obj.backrefs holds the references pointing the other way, so the graph can be walked in both directions.\n\nUse properties rather than the properties field to also see what extensions in other profiles added.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.CIMObject"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.CIMExtension","page":"API","text":"CIMExtension\n\nExtra properties that a second profile attaches to an object defined elsewhere — the SteadyStateHypothesis profile restating an Equipment object to give it an operating point, for instance. The extension is linked to its base object, and properties merges the two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.CIMExtension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.CIMRef","page":"API","text":"CIMRef(id::String)\n\nA reference from one object to another, by UUID. References start out unresolved and get their target filled in by resolve_references!.\n\nReferences to the CIM schema itself (enum values, written as http://... URIs) stay unresolved on purpose — see is_external_ref.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.CIMRef"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.CIMBackref","page":"API","text":"CIMBackref\n\nThe reverse side of a reference: recorded on the target object so you can ask what points at it. source is the object holding the reference and prop the property it lives in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.CIMBackref"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.resolve_references!","page":"API","text":"resolve_references!(collection; warn=true)\n\nPoint every reference in collection at its target and record the matching backreference, so the objects can be walked in both directions.\n\nExisting links are cleared first, which is what makes this safe to re-run after objects have been added or deleted. References that cannot be resolved are marked as such rather than being an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.resolve_references!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.merge_collection","page":"API","text":"merge_collection(c1, c2; warn=true, metadatakey=:merger_of)\n\nCombine two collections into a new one and resolve the references across the seam. Warns about objects and metadata the two disagree on. The result remembers its parts under metadatakey.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.merge_collection"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.objects","page":"API","text":"objects(collection) -> OrderedDict{String, CIMObject}\n\nAll objects in a collection, dataset or file, keyed by UUID.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.objects"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.extensions","page":"API","text":"extensions(collection) -> Vector{CIMExtension}\n\nAll extensions in a collection, dataset or file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.extensions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.properties","page":"API","text":"properties(obj::CIMObject)\n\nEverything known about obj, including what extensions in other profiles added to it. This is almost always what you want; the obj.properties field only holds what the object's own profile said.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.properties"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.getname","page":"API","text":"getname(obj)\n\nThe human readable name of an object. Throws if it has none — see hasname.\n\nNames are what you usually match on when comparing two exports of the same grid, since the UUIDs differ between them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.getname"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.hasname","page":"API","text":"hasname(obj)\n\nWhether an object carries a name. Many CGMES classes do, but bookkeeping classes such as PositionPoint do not, so check this before reaching for getname.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.hasname"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.follow_ref","page":"API","text":"follow_ref(x)\n\nResolve a reference to the object it points at. Passes anything that is not a reference straight through, which makes it safe to apply to a property value of unknown type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.follow_ref"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.base_object","page":"API","text":"base_object(x)\n\nFollows references, in case of extensions also returns the base object being extended.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.base_object"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.is_class","page":"API","text":"is_class(x, class)\nis_class(class)  # x |> is_class(class)\n\nWhether x is a CIMObject of the given class. class can be a name, a regex, or a list of either. The single argument form returns a predicate, which is handy for filter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.is_class"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.descend","page":"API","text":"descend(obj, matcher)\ndescend(matcher) # obj |> descend(matcher)\n\nFollow forward references to find exactly one matching CIMObject (verb form).\n\nReturns a single CIMObject. Errors if 0 or multiple matches are found. Matcher can be byprop or byclass.\n\nSee also: descendants() to get all matches without error checking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.descend"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.descendants","page":"API","text":"descendants(obj, matcher)\ndescendants(matcher) # obj |> descendants(matcher)\n\nGet all forward references matching the criteria (plural noun form).\n\nReturns a Vector{CIMObject} containing all matching descendants (0 or more). Matcher can be byprop or byclass.\n\nSee also: descend() to get exactly one match with error checking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.descendants"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.ascend","page":"API","text":"ascend(obj, matcher)\nascend(matcher) # obj |> ascend(matcher)\n\nFollow backward references to find exactly one matching CIMObject (verb form).\n\nReturns a single CIMObject. Errors if 0 or multiple matches are found. Matcher can be byprop or byclass.\n\nSee also: ascendants() to get all matches without error checking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.ascend"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.ascendants","page":"API","text":"ascendants(obj, matcher)\nascendants(matcher) # obj |> ascendants(matcher)\n\nGet all backward references matching the criteria (plural noun form).\n\nReturns a Vector{CIMObject} containing all matching ascendants (0 or more). Matcher can be byprop or byclass.\n\nSee also: ascend() to get exactly one match with error checking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.ascendants"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.byclass","page":"API","text":"byclass(class; via=nothing)\n\nMatcher selecting relations by the class of the object at the other end. Pass via to also require that the reference sits in a particular property, which is how you distinguish several references between the same pair of classes.\n\nUsed together with descend, ascend and their plural forms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.byclass"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.byprop","page":"API","text":"byprop(prop)\n\nMatcher selecting relations by the name of the property the reference lives in. prop can be a string or a regex.\n\nUsed together with descend, ascend and their plural forms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.byprop"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.symbolify","page":"API","text":"symbolify(s::String)\n\nTurn a CGMES object name into a Symbol usable as a component name, replacing whitespace with underscores.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.symbolify"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.rdf_node","page":"API","text":"Extract the \"Rescource Description Framework\" (RDF) node from the XML document.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.rdf_node"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.Relation","page":"API","text":"Relation(this, other, property)\n\nModels a relationship between two CIMObjects via a property. This works for both forward and backward references!\n\nForward ref:  this[property] -> other Backward ref: this <- other[property]\n\nIt is used as collections for this to follow to other.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.Relation"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#CGMESParser.@hover","page":"API","text":"@hover expr\n\nNo-op in normal use. The documentation build rewrites it to append an HTML hover map after the plot produced by expr, so that tooltips survive into the static docs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.@hover"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.split_topologically","page":"API","text":"split_topologically(collection; verbose=false, warn=false) -> (buses, branches)\n\nSplit a dataset into the electrical graph it describes: one subgraph per bus and one per branch between two buses. Both are returned as CIMCollections holding the objects that belong to them, so anything you can do to a dataset you can also do to a single bus.\n\nSwitching detail is collapsed on the way — closed breakers merge the nodes they join, chains of branch elements become a single branch. Each branch subgraph records the names of the buses it connects in its metadata under :src_name and :dst_name, and each bus records its index under :busidx; a branch built from several elements keeps them under :branches.\n\nErrors on equipment classes it cannot classify, which is usually how you find out a dataset contains something not yet supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.split_topologically"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.reduce_complexity","page":"API","text":"reduce_complexity(collection, filter_classes=String[]; del_uncon=true)\n\nDrop the classes that carry no electrical meaning — diagram layout, geographical position, operational limits, base voltages — and then anything left unconnected. Mostly useful to get a picture small enough to actually look at.\n\nPass extra class names in filter_classes to remove more.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.reduce_complexity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.delete_unconnected","page":"API","text":"delete_unconnected(collection, keep=collection(\"TopologicalNode\"); warn=true)\n\nRemove objects that are no longer reachable from keep, following references in either direction. Filtering a collection tends to leave such orphans behind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.delete_unconnected"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.to_digraph","page":"API","text":"to_digraph(collection) -> (nodes, graph)\n\nThe collection as a plain SimpleDiGraph, one vertex per object and one edge per resolved reference. nodes gives the object belonging to each vertex index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.to_digraph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.discover_subgraph","page":"API","text":"discover_subgraph(root; kwargs)::CIMCollection\n\nkeyword arguments\n\nnobackref = is_class(STOP_BACKREF): don't follow back references on those nodes  unless the backref is from a :StateVariables profile\nmaxdepth = 100: maximum depth to explore\nfilter_out = x -> false: filter out nodes that match this predicate\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.discover_subgraph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.is_terminal","page":"API","text":"is_terminal(t)\n\nWhether t is a Terminal object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.is_terminal"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#CGMESParser.SBASE","page":"API","text":"SBASE[]\n\nSystem base power in MVA used to convert the MW/MVAr values in the CGMES files to per-unit. CGMES itself carries no system base, so this is a convention of this package — set it before reading powers if your dataset assumes a different one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.SBASE"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.get_base_voltage","page":"API","text":"get_base_voltage(ob::CIMObject) -> kV\n\nNominal voltage of the object, in kV. Terminals take the base voltage of the node they sit on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.get_base_voltage"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.get_voltage_pu","page":"API","text":"get_voltage_pu(o) -> Complex\n\nVoltage at a node, terminal or bus subgraph, as a complex phasor in per unit of the node's base voltage. Read from the SvVoltage in the StateVariables profile, so this is the operating point the exporting tool computed, not something recomputed here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.get_voltage_pu"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.get_injected_power_pu","page":"API","text":"ATTENTION: we go from load to injector convention\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.get_injected_power_pu"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.get_connecting_terminal","page":"API","text":"get_connecting_terminal(injector::CIMObject)\n\nThe Terminal through which a piece of equipment is attached to the grid. Errors unless there is exactly one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.get_connecting_terminal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.is_angle_ref","page":"API","text":"is_angle_ref(o::CIMObject)\n\nWhether a TopologicalNode is the angle reference of its island — the slack bus, in powerflow terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.is_angle_ref"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.in_service","page":"API","text":"in_service(inj)\n\nWhether a piece of equipment (or the terminal it hangs on) is switched in. Both the Equipment profile and an SvStatus in the StateVariables profile can say, and this errors if the two disagree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.in_service"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.injector_type","page":"API","text":"injector_type(o::CIMObject) -> Injector\n\nRead what a piece of equipment does to the bus it hangs on, as one of SlackType, PVType or PQYType. Powers come out in per unit and in injector convention, so a load is negative.\n\nDispatches on the CIM class; generators, loads, power electronics and shunt compensators are handled. Several injectors on one bus can be reduced with combine.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.injector_type"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.Injector","page":"API","text":"Injector\n\nWhat a piece of equipment does to the bus it hangs on, in powerflow terms: SlackType, PVType or PQYType. Several injectors on the same bus reduce to one with combine, which is where the usual precedence applies — a slack absorbs everything, a PV bus keeps its voltage and picks up the extra power.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.Injector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.SlackType","page":"API","text":"SlackType(V, objs)\n\nA bus that holds both voltage magnitude and angle. V is NaN when the node is only the angle reference and nothing fixes its magnitude.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.SlackType"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.PVType","page":"API","text":"PVType(P, V, objs)\n\nA bus holding active power and voltage magnitude, in per unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.PVType"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.PQYType","page":"API","text":"PQYType(P, Q, G, B, objs)\n\nA bus with a fixed power injection plus a shunt admittance, all in per unit. Plain loads and generators leave G and B at zero; a pure shunt compensator leaves P and Q at zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.PQYType"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.classify_branch_subgraph","page":"API","text":"classify_branch_subgraph(c) -> AbstractEdgeSubgraph or nothing\n\nWhat kind of branch a two-bus subgraph describes: an ACLineSegment, a PowerTransformer, a Breaker, or a MultiBranchSubgraph when several elements sit in series. Returns nothing for anything else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.classify_branch_subgraph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.filter_loopback_breakers","page":"API","text":"filter_loopback_breakers(data) -> CIMCollection\n\nRemove breakers and switches whose two terminals sit on the same topological node. They carry no current and only get in the way of the topological split.\n\nWarns when such a breaker was marked as retained, or when the powerflow stored on its two terminals does not cancel out — either is a sign the export is not quite consistent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.filter_loopback_breakers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.merge_tpn_on_breakers","page":"API","text":"merge_tpn_on_breakers(data) -> CIMCollection\n\nMerge topological nodes that share a name and are joined by a closed breaker, collapsing them into one bus. Exports that model busbar detail tend to hand out one node per busbar section, all carrying the same name.\n\nSee rename_dangling_tpn for the other way of dealing with duplicate node names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.merge_tpn_on_breakers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.reattach_regulating_control","page":"API","text":"reattach_regulating_control(data) -> CIMCollection\n\nPoint each RegulatingControl at the terminal of the equipment it actually regulates. Some exports attach it to a different terminal on the same node, which is harmless for the powerflow but confusing to read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.reattach_regulating_control"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.rename_dangling_tpn","page":"API","text":"rename_dangling_tpn(data) -> CIMCollection\n\nGive unique names to topological nodes that share a name with another node. Only nodes with a single neighbour are renamed, by prefixing that neighbour's name.\n\nDuplicate node names can also be a sign that a node was split across a breaker, in which case merge_tpn_on_breakers is the remedy rather than this one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.rename_dangling_tpn"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CGMESParser.CIMCollectionComparison","page":"API","text":"CIMCollectionComparison(A::CIMCollection, B::CIMCollection)\n\nMatch up the objects of two collections, so that two exports of the same grid can be compared. Objects are matched on their class, properties and what they reference — not on UUID, which differs between exports.\n\nThe result holds the matching in both directions. Objects that are genuinely identical to one another (duplicate limits, position points) can be matched ambiguously; that is warned about but not an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.CIMCollectionComparison"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.compare_objects","page":"API","text":"compare_objects(obj1, obj2; io=stdout)\n\nPrint the properties of two objects side by side, marking what differs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.compare_objects"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.inspect_collection","page":"API","text":"inspect_collection(collection; filter_out=String[], size=(2000,1500), hl1=[], hl2=[],\n                   seed=1, edge_labels=true, node_labels=:long)\n\nPlot every object in collection as a node and every resolved reference as an edge, with nodes colored by the profile they came from. filter_out drops classes whose name contains any of the given strings, which is how you get a readable picture out of a full dataset.\n\nRequires GraphMakie and a Makie backend to be loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.inspect_collection"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.inspect_node","page":"API","text":"inspect_node(node::CIMObject; stop_classes=String[], filter_out=String[], max_depth=100, ...)\n\nPlot the neighbourhood of a single object, walking references outwards until hitting a class in stop_classes or max_depth. Useful for asking \"what is attached to this Terminal\".\n\nRequires GraphMakie and a Makie backend to be loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.inspect_node"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.inspect_comparison","page":"API","text":"inspect_comparison(comparison::CIMCollectionComparison; size=(2000,1000), ...)\n\nPlot two collections side by side, graying out the objects that matched and pinning them to the same positions, so that whatever differs stands out.\n\nRequires GraphMakie and a Makie backend to be loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.inspect_comparison"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CGMESParser.html_hover_map","page":"API","text":"html_hover_map(fig=Makie.current_figure())\n\nWrap fig in an HTML image map carrying the per-node tooltips, so hovering works in a static documentation build where no live Makie backend is running.\n\nRequires GraphMakie and a Makie backend to be loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CGMESParser.html_hover_map"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/inspecting_a_dataset/#Inspecting-a-Dataset","page":"Inspecting a Dataset","text":"Real CGMES exports are large and full of detail that has nothing to do with the electrical model. This page walks through meeting an unfamiliar dataset: read it, strip it down to something you can look at, and then split it into buses and branches.\n\nusing CGMESParser\nusing CairoMakie\nusing GraphMakie\n\nDATA = joinpath(pkgdir(CGMESParser), \"test\", \"data\", \"1-EHVHV-mixed-all-2-sw-Ausschnitt\")\ndataset = CIMCollection(CIMDataset(DATA))","title":"Inspecting a Dataset"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/inspecting_a_dataset/#Getting-a-first-picture","page":"Inspecting a Dataset","text":"Plotting the whole collection shows every object and every reference between them. Nodes are colored by the profile they came from.\n\ntip: Tip\nHover the nodes to inspect their properties!\n\nreduce_complexity drops the classes that carry no electrical meaning — diagram layout, geographical position, operational limits — which is usually the difference between an unreadable hairball and a picture.\n\nreduced_dataset = reduce_complexity(dataset)\ninspect_collection(reduced_dataset; edge_labels=false, node_labels=:short, size=(1000,1000))\n\nCGMESParser.html_hover_map() #hide","title":"Getting a first picture"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/inspecting_a_dataset/#Splitting-into-buses-and-branches","page":"Inspecting a Dataset","text":"Still too much to take in at once. split_topologically collapses the switching detail and hands back two lists of subgraphs: one per bus, one per branch. Each subgraph is a full CIMCollection, so everything you can do to a dataset you can also do to a single bus.\n\nnodes, edges = split_topologically(dataset; warn=false)\nlength(nodes), length(edges)","title":"Splitting into buses and branches"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/inspecting_a_dataset/#A-single-bus","page":"Inspecting a Dataset","text":"Three synchronous machines on one topological node:\n\ninspect_collection(nodes[1]; size=(900,900))\n\nCGMESParser.html_hover_map() #hide\n\nA load:\n\ninspect_collection(nodes[3]; size=(900,900))\n\nCGMESParser.html_hover_map() #hide","title":"A single bus"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/inspecting_a_dataset/#A-single-branch","page":"Inspecting a Dataset","text":"A transformer. Both transformer ends carry r/x and g/b, so this can be read as a pi-line with two voltage bases:\n\ninspect_collection(edges[1]; size=(900,900))\n\nCGMESParser.html_hover_map() #hide\n\nAnd an ordinary AC line:\n\ninspect_collection(edges[2]; size=(900,900))\n\nCGMESParser.html_hover_map() #hide\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"A single branch"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/walking_the_graph/#Walking-the-Object-Graph","page":"Walking the Object Graph","text":"A parsed dataset is an object graph: every CIMObject carries the properties from the XML, and the UUID references between objects are resolved in both directions. This page covers how to get from one object to another.\n\nusing CGMESParser\n\nDATA = joinpath(pkgdir(CGMESParser), \"test\", \"data\", \"testdata1\")\ndataset = CIMDataset(DATA)","title":"Walking the Object Graph"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/walking_the_graph/#Finding-objects-by-class","page":"Walking the Object Graph","text":"Calling a collection with a class name gives you every object of that class. A regex or a vector of names works too.\n\ndataset(\"TopologicalNode\")","title":"Finding objects by class"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/walking_the_graph/#Following-a-reference","page":"Walking the Object Graph","text":"Indexing an object with a property name follows the reference and returns the object on the other end, so chains read the way the model does.\n\nterminal = first(dataset(\"Terminal\"))\nterminal[\"TopologicalNode\"]\n\nProperties that are plain values come back as values. properties(obj) returns everything including what extensions in other profiles added — the SSH profile, for instance, restates Equipment objects to attach the operating point, and those show up here but not in the raw obj.properties field.\n\nproperties(terminal)","title":"Following a reference"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/walking_the_graph/#Going-against-the-direction-of-a-reference","page":"Walking the Object Graph","text":"References in CGMES point one way, but the interesting question is often the other way round: not \"which node does this terminal sit on\" but \"what is attached to this node\". That is what ascendants is for, with descendants being the forward equivalent.\n\nBoth take a matcher. byclass selects on the class of the object at the other end, with an optional via to also pin down which property the reference lives in; byprop selects on the property name alone.\n\nnode = terminal[\"TopologicalNode\"]\nascendants(node, byclass(\"Terminal\", via=\"TopologicalNode\"))\n\ndescend and ascend are the singular forms: same matchers, but they return the object itself and error unless there is exactly one match. Use them when the model guarantees a single answer and you would rather find out immediately when it does not.\n\ndescend(terminal, byclass(\"TopologicalNode\"))","title":"Going against the direction of a reference"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/walking_the_graph/#Reading-the-stored-operating-point","page":"Walking the Object Graph","text":"The SV profile carries the powerflow the exporting tool computed. The semantic accessors read it in per unit, using CGMESParser.SBASE as the system base — CGMES itself carries no system base, so this is a convention of this package.\n\nPowers use the injector convention: a load comes out negative.\n\nusing CGMESParser: get_voltage_pu, get_injected_power_pu, get_base_voltage, get_connecting_terminal\n\nget_base_voltage(node), get_voltage_pu(node)\n\nload = first(dataset(\"ConformLoad\"))\nget_injected_power_pu(get_connecting_terminal(load))\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Reading the stored operating point"},{"category":"section","location":"#CGMESParser","page":"Home","text":"Reading, exploring and visualizing CGMES (Common Grid Model Exchange Standard) datasets in Julia.\n\nA CGMES model is a directory of XML files, one per profile, whose objects reference each other by UUID. CGMESParser reads such a directory into an object graph you can walk in both directions, and adds the layers you would otherwise write yourself: cleanup passes for the quirks real exports contain, a split into buses and branches, and per-unit accessors for the powerflow stored in the StateVariables profile.\n\nThe package knows nothing about any simulation framework. It gives you the grid as data.","title":"CGMESParser"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reading-a-dataset","page":"Home","text":"CIMDataset reads a whole directory and resolves the references between all profiles. It is a faithful image of what the files say — nothing is cleaned up or interpreted at this stage.\n\nusing CGMESParser\ndataset = CIMDataset(\"path/to/cgmes/directory\")\n\nFrom there, three optional layers sit on top:\n\nWalking the Object Graph — finding objects and following references.\nInspecting a Dataset — plotting the object graph, and splitting the model into buses and branches.\nCleanup — filter_loopback_breakers, merge_tpn_on_breakers, reattach_regulating_control and rename_dangling_tpn each take a collection and return a corrected copy. They are deliberately not applied automatically: they change what the file said, and which of them you want depends on the export.","title":"Reading a dataset"},{"category":"section","location":"#Plotting","page":"Home","text":"Visualization lives in a package extension so that the parser itself has no plotting dependency. Load GraphMakie together with a Makie backend to enable it:\n\nusing GraphMakie, CairoMakie\ninspect_collection(dataset)","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-is-interpreted","page":"Home","text":"Every class parses, but only some are given electrical meaning by the semantic layer: Terminal, TopologicalNode, ACLineSegment, PowerTransformer, Breaker, SynchronousMachine, ConformLoad, PowerElectronicsConnection and LinearShuntCompensator. Everything else is readable as data but will not be classified by split_topologically or the injector accessors.","title":"What is interpreted"}]
}
